                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="759-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="759-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="759-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ><img id="759-3" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_xy.png" width=27 height=27 alt="X/Y Stereo" title="X/Y Stereo" ><img id="759-4" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_ms.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Mid-Side Stereo" title="Mid-Side Stereo" ><img id="759-5" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_bl.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Blumlein" title="Blumlein" ></span>Studio Projects LSD2</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Stereo Microphone</h2>
                    <p>Studio Projects&#8217; LSD2 is a stereo multipattern FET condenser microphone with transformerless output.</p><p>Two 27mm capsules are mounted atop one another. The lower capsule is fixed in place; the top capsule can be rotated 270&deg; relative to the lower, allowing an infinite variety of stereo options.</p><p>Both capsules are dual-diaphragm, multipattern designs, capable of being switched between Cardioid, Omni, and Figure-of-8 responses. Setting both to Cardioid, offset by 90&deg; produces a typical coincident-pair or XY stereo response. Setting both to figure-8 would produce a Blumlein response. In both cases, the stereo field can be modified by adjusting the angle of separation or the pickup pattern of either capsule.</p><p>The mic can also produce a mid-side response, by setting the front-facing (&#8220;mid&#8221;) capsule to Cardioid, and adjusting the top capsule to figure-8 at 90&deg; (&#8220;side&#8221;).</p><blockquote><p><cite>Electronic Musician</cite></p><p>The guitar sound that I recorded with the LSD2 in an M-S configuration was nothing short of brilliant.</p></blockquote><p>A pad, a high-pass filter, and the pattern selection for each capsule is controlled by switches mounted on the microphone body. A pair of switches on the front provide access to the pad/HPF (one switch) and pattern (second switch) for the lower, fixed capsule. A duplicate pair of switches on the rear of the mic body control the top capsule.</p><p>The LSD2 kit includes the mic, a 25&#0039; Y-cable (5-pin female XLR to two XLR3M, one per capsule), an elastic shockmount, and a foam windscreen; it ships in a padded hard case.</p><p>The LSD2 was introduced at Winter NAMM 2003; the mic began shipping in March of 2003 at an MSRP of $999.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Studio-Projects/LSD2">Studio Projects LSD2</a> is also known as: LSD-2.</p><p>The mic was released in 2003.</p>
